WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.250
Instructor: All right, so as the slide says,

00:02.250 --> 00:05.670
it's time for a quick, high-level conceptual overview.

00:05.670 --> 00:06.780
I'd like to try and

00:06.780 --> 00:08.670
introduce things at a conceptual level first

00:08.670 --> 00:10.350
before we dive into the commands.

00:10.350 --> 00:11.670
But I also don't want to take too long.

00:11.670 --> 00:14.070
I know we just want to get to the commands.

00:14.070 --> 00:17.670
So in this video I want to talk about the term "repository".

00:17.670 --> 00:19.410
This comes up all the time when we talk

00:19.410 --> 00:21.510
about Git and GitHub.

00:21.510 --> 00:24.270
So much in fact that people usually just say "repo"

00:24.270 --> 00:26.100
instead of "repository".

00:26.100 --> 00:27.780
Save a couple of characters there.

00:27.780 --> 00:29.040
They're the same thing.

00:29.040 --> 00:32.280
So a Git repository is just a Git workspace.

00:32.280 --> 00:34.650
Installing Git on our machine is great.

00:34.650 --> 00:36.570
You know, I have Git over here.

00:36.570 --> 00:38.283
Hopefully you installed Git,

00:39.180 --> 00:41.850
following the instructions in the course.

00:41.850 --> 00:43.860
I have Git, I can just verify it's here.

00:43.860 --> 00:45.000
I can check the version.

00:45.000 --> 00:45.833
Sure.

00:45.833 --> 00:49.440
But that doesn't mean Git is active or that I'm using Git.

00:49.440 --> 00:51.360
It's just on my machine.

00:51.360 --> 00:54.690
I have to manually tell Git when I want to use it

00:54.690 --> 00:59.100
and in which directories, I have to create repositories.

00:59.100 --> 01:01.290
So anytime I want to use Git for

01:01.290 --> 01:03.810
let's say I'm writing a new symphony or a novel,

01:03.810 --> 01:06.330
or I'm working on a freelance web development project,

01:06.330 --> 01:07.860
or whatever it is,

01:07.860 --> 01:11.280
I need to tell Git to create a new repository.

01:11.280 --> 01:14.700
So it's just a Git workspace with it's own history.

01:14.700 --> 01:17.610
So every Git repo has its own history.

01:17.610 --> 01:19.920
They're not linked, they're not connected in any way.

01:19.920 --> 01:22.500
They're these like little encapsulated bubbles.

01:22.500 --> 01:24.570
That's why I have this jar on the right

01:24.570 --> 01:26.490
with some Git history inside of it.

01:26.490 --> 01:28.920
So we can have as many repos as needed,

01:28.920 --> 01:32.160
all with separate histories and contents on our machine.

01:32.160 --> 01:33.900
So I may have a bunch of folders,

01:33.900 --> 01:34.920
a bunch of different projects.

01:34.920 --> 01:37.290
Each one of these is a separate project.

01:37.290 --> 01:41.310
If I want to use Git, I have to go into each project folder

01:41.310 --> 01:43.770
and create a Git repository.

01:43.770 --> 01:47.160
I have to tell Git, "All right, I want to repo in here,

01:47.160 --> 01:50.430
and in here, and in here, and in here."

01:50.430 --> 01:52.320
And whatever happens in those folders,

01:52.320 --> 01:55.980
whatever Git commands I run, whatever history I store

01:55.980 --> 01:59.940
with Git, they're completely separate and not connected.

01:59.940 --> 02:02.910
So this allows me to have different histories for

02:02.910 --> 02:07.170
a symphony, an app I'm cloning, a movie script I'm writing,

02:07.170 --> 02:10.470
a startup idea, a whatever, a novel, portfolio website,

02:10.470 --> 02:11.303
you name it,

02:11.303 --> 02:14.100
I can have as many separate repositories as I need.

02:14.100 --> 02:17.670
And to wrap things up, here I have two separate folders,

02:17.670 --> 02:20.010
two different projects on my machine,

02:20.010 --> 02:22.920
and I've created Git repositories in each of them,

02:22.920 --> 02:24.810
which I haven't shown you how to do just yet,

02:24.810 --> 02:25.770
but I just want to show you

02:25.770 --> 02:27.300
that they have separate histories.

02:27.300 --> 02:30.660
So one project is this large web application,

02:30.660 --> 02:32.340
a bunch of files and folders.

02:32.340 --> 02:35.670
This other one, so the first one is called "Yelp Camp 2.0".

02:35.670 --> 02:37.770
It's a project for one of my courses.

02:37.770 --> 02:39.060
The other one is just "Animals".

02:39.060 --> 02:40.680
It's a single folder with one file,

02:40.680 --> 02:43.440
very straightforward, just a list of animals.

02:43.440 --> 02:45.630
They both have separate Git histories.

02:45.630 --> 02:47.880
They are distinct repositories.

02:47.880 --> 02:49.710
I can prove that to you in a couple of ways.

02:49.710 --> 02:51.990
I'll show you in the console here.

02:51.990 --> 02:54.810
Can see all of this history here is associated

02:54.810 --> 02:57.360
with this "Yelp Camp" repository.

02:57.360 --> 02:58.470
So lots of stuff.

02:58.470 --> 03:00.420
What that stuff is, how I see it,

03:00.420 --> 03:02.100
don't worry about that.

03:02.100 --> 03:04.890
If I go to the "Animals" directory and I do the same thing,

03:04.890 --> 03:07.140
a lot less and different history,

03:07.140 --> 03:09.480
all the histories about animals.

03:09.480 --> 03:11.670
Another way to show that to you is through a GUI,

03:11.670 --> 03:12.930
like GitKraken.

03:12.930 --> 03:15.180
Here, I'm looking at the "Animals" history

03:15.180 --> 03:17.040
for this one repository.

03:17.040 --> 03:19.560
You can see, you know, there's a decent amount of stuff.

03:19.560 --> 03:22.020
It all has to do with animal-y stuff.

03:22.020 --> 03:25.260
And if I switch over to a different repository,

03:25.260 --> 03:28.440
very different, completely distinct history.

03:28.440 --> 03:29.430
There's collaborators.

03:29.430 --> 03:30.510
It's not just me.

03:30.510 --> 03:33.570
And this is, it goes back like a year or more,

03:33.570 --> 03:37.320
versus this animals one is a lot more recent

03:37.320 --> 03:39.750
and obviously focused on animals.

03:39.750 --> 03:41.940
Anyway, we'll learn more about what all this is

03:41.940 --> 03:44.640
and how we make these checkpoints and how to use the GUI

03:44.640 --> 03:48.000
and the command line and all that stuff in upcoming videos.

03:48.000 --> 03:50.490
But for now, I just wanted to explain this concept

03:50.490 --> 03:53.700
of repositories and just prove, show you some evidence

03:53.700 --> 03:56.250
of different Git histories for two different repos.

03:56.250 --> 03:57.570
So that's all there is to this term,

03:57.570 --> 03:59.250
this concept of a repository.

03:59.250 --> 04:00.660
It's a Git workspace.

04:00.660 --> 04:03.150
And in the next video I'll show you how we create them,

04:03.150 --> 04:04.893
how we instantiate a new repo.
